low-latency = Low latency
balanced = Balanced
high = High
very-high = Very high
answers-about-a-millisecond-after-the = Answers about a millisecond after the sound goes in, where the others wait tens of milliseconds, for the most work of the four and a treble starting to fall a little below the top of the band. Used only where the file's rate has to be changed.
the-least-work-of-the-four = The least work of the four, with errors far below what a 16-bit file can hold. Used only where the file's rate has to be changed.
a-sharper-filter-whose-errors-sit = A sharper filter whose errors sit below what a 24-bit file can hold, for a little more work than Balanced. Used only where the file's rate has to be changed.
the-sharpest-filter-keeping-the-treble = The sharpest filter, keeping the treble flat almost to the top of the band and its errors below what any file holds. It waits longest for its first sound; used only where the file's rate has to be changed.
linear = Linear
intermediate = Intermediate
minimum = Minimum
every-frequency-is-delayed-alike-so = Every frequency is delayed alike, so nothing is shifted against anything else; what rings, rings a little before a sharp sound as well as after it.
halfway-between-the-two-less-ringing = Halfway between the two: less ringing ahead of a sharp sound than linear, with only a small shift at the top of the treble.
nothing-rings-before-a-sharp-sound = Nothing rings before a sharp sound, all of it after, at the cost of the top of the treble arriving a touch later than the rest.
off = Off
repair = Repair
repair-and-extend = Repair and extend
mp3-aac-and-vorbis-files-play = MP3, AAC and Vorbis files play exactly as they decode, with the treble the encoder cut away left missing.
undoes-the-damage-an-encoder-leaves = Undoes the damage an encoder leaves under its cutoff and adds nothing above it: the treble it turned down just below the cutoff is lifted back, and short gaps it punched in the upper treble are filled with matching noise.
repairs-as-above-then-rebuilds-the = Repairs as above, then rebuilds the treble the encoder threw away above its cutoff — usually from 16 to 19 kHz upwards — out of the octave beneath it, fading away as it climbs. It puts back an air the file no longer holds, which is a guess at what was there rather than what was there.
none = None
rectangular = Rectangular
triangular = Triangular
where-the-bits-have-to-be = Where the bits have to be cut down, they are rounded, which leaves a faint distortion that follows the music in quiet passages.
a-whisper-of-noise-is-added = A whisper of noise is added before the bits are cut down, turning that distortion into steady hiss, though the hiss still rises and falls with the music.
twice-the-whisper-which-leaves-only = Twice the whisper, which leaves only a steady, even hiss far below the music and nothing that follows it. The usual choice.
flat = Flat
threshold = Threshold
the-dithers-hiss-is-spread-evenly = The dither's hiss is spread evenly across every frequency, the ones the ear is keenest on included.
an-e-weighted-fit-to-the = An E-weighted fit to the ear's sensitivity, designed at 44.1 kHz, which pushes the hiss up where the ear is deaf to it. It runs at 44.1 and 48 kHz and falls back to flat dither at every other rate.
designed-at-the-devices-own-rate = Designed at the device's own rate from the threshold of hearing, so it shapes at every rate. The noise it leaves where the ear can hear it is 7 dB quieter than Lipshitz's at 44.1 kHz, and 26 dB quieter than flat dither at 96 kHz.
light = Light
moderate = Moderate
strong = Strong
each-channel-stays-in-its-own = Each channel stays in its own ear, exactly as the record was mixed.
a-faint-low-copy-of-each = A faint, low copy of each side reaches the other ear (650 Hz, 9.5 dB down), enough to take the edge off a hard-panned record.
the-classic-setting-700-hz-6 = The classic setting (700 Hz, 6 dB down): the image closes up toward the front without losing its width.
the-most-of-the-other-side = The most of the other side reaches each ear (700 Hz, 4.5 dB down), for old stereo records with the voices on one side and the band on the other.
track = Track
album = Album
every-track-plays-at-the-level = Every track plays at the level it was mastered at.
each-track-is-brought-to-one = Each track is brought to one loudness by its own ReplayGain tag, so a shuffle across albums does not jump in level.
each-album-is-brought-to-one = Each album is brought to one loudness as a whole, so the quiet songs on it stay quieter than the loud ones, as the record was made. A track tagged with no album gain uses its own.
crossfeed-stereo-only = Only a stereo stream is crossfed; anything else plays as it is whatever this says. A crossfed stream is converted rather than bit-perfect.
lossy-only = Only MP3, AAC and Vorbis files are touched; a lossless file plays as it is whatever this says. A file that is repaired is converted rather than bit-perfect, and the cutoff is read off the file's own study where there is one, or heard as it plays.
lowered-6-db = −6 dB
lowered-3-db = −3 dB
raised-3-db = +3 dB
raised-6-db = +6 dB
lowered-9-db = −9 dB
added-to-every-gain-a-replaygain = Added to every gain a ReplayGain tag asks for. Clip prevention still holds the result under the peak the tag declares.
the-gain-a-track-carrying-no = The gain a track carrying no ReplayGain tag is played at, so it sits nearer the tagged tracks around it.
phase = Phase
keep-true-peaks-under-full-scale = Keep true peaks under full scale
turn-a-track-down-by-its = Turn a track down by its measured peak and guard what is processed
pre-amp = Pre-amp
without-tags = Without tags
filter-in-blocks = Converted a block at a time through Fourier transforms: a filter reaching { $half_taps } samples either side of each output instant, its window shaped to ring for as short a time as its depth allows and silent from the lower rate's Nyquist. Cutoff { $cutoff } of Nyquist.
filter-in-phases = { $half_taps } source samples either side of each output instant — { $taps } taps where the device's rate is at or above the file's, and more in proportion below it. { $phases } phases in the table those weights are read from. Cutoff { $cutoff } of Nyquist, Kaiser β { $beta }.
